{
  "version": 1,
  "about": "Memory entry op batches. Each case gives `entries`, the store's entries before, each with all ten fields (id, topic, text, created_at, updated_at, valid_until, source, locked, source_conversation_id, hidden_at), and `ops`, one batch, applied in order at `now`. `expect.entries` is every entry afterwards, in the store's order, hidden ones included: a removed or forgotten entry stays, with hidden_at `now`, and one passed in hidden is carried through as it is, unseen by every op. `expect.dropped` is the rule name of each op dropped, and \"locked\" for each locked entry a forget passes by, in order. An added entry goes last, with the next id of a counter (e1, e2, ...) that passes over ids in use, created_at and updated_at `now` and source \"said\"; an id on an add is ignored. Every text cap counts Unicode code points, never graphemes (Swift's String.count) or UTF-16 units (JavaScript's .length): an entry's text is 1 to 300 of them as written, before any normalisation, and a forget's phrase at least 2 once normalised (NFKC, casefolded, spaces collapsed, trailing .!?,;: removed). A forget hides each unlocked visible entry that holds its phrase as whole words: the phrase's index tokens, in order among the entry's, the last allowed an \"s\" or \"es\" after it, whatever that token is (\"Sam\" hides \"Sames\" too). Whole words, not a substring: a substring would also hide \"same\" and \"Samsung\" for \"Sam\", and the user would not see what went. Index tokens are normalise_for_index's text (NFKC, casefolded, combining marks removed, each CJK or Thai character set apart) as unicode61 with remove_diacritics 2 parts it: runs of letters, numbers and private-use characters, a Latin letter without its diacritics, so an apostrophe, straight or curly, parts \"Sam's\". A phrase with no tokens, or only tokens in word_lists.json's forget_vague_words, is too vague, a rule checked after the length: it names nothing, so it would hide much more than the user meant, and \"forget it\" is everyday speech for \"never mind\". A batch has no length limit on the device: update_memory's 1 to 5 ops are the engine's rule, and extraction's entry_ops have none (sixth_op_applied). Python's apply_entry_ops and MemoryStore.apply_ops replay every case (talk2view-python, tests/test_contract_memory.py).",
